\paragraph{Transferable diagnostic protocol.}
The four-level intervention hierarchy (gradient $\to$ output $\to$ representation $\to$ architecture) constitutes a reusable diagnostic protocol, not a one-off failure report. The protocol does not presuppose which component is at fault; it systematically narrows the locus by testing increasingly coarse interventions. Gradient-level probes (SGS) reveal pathway redundancy; output-level probes (RAVE) distinguish value-side from representation-side pathologies; representation-level probes (TCN) expose dose--effect tradeoffs; architecture-level tests (dual critics) establish whether the failure is upstream of estimation error. This layered elimination applies whenever a learned conditioning signal interacts with imagination-based training---goal-conditioned imagination, language-conditioned world models, or skill embeddings each introduce auxiliary representations that could co-adapt with the dynamics model through analogous gradient pathways.

