Table~\ref{tab:cross_env_civo} establishes both the generality and the boundary of CIVO through a three-tier evidence structure. \emph{Tier 1 (replication):} In multi-joint environments (Walker Walk, 6 joints; Cheetah Run, 6 joints), the trigger configuration reliably induces CIVO (NVE $4.72$--$8.27\times$) while the no-embedding control does not (NVE $\leq 1.69\times$, no value overestimation$^*$); the trained $\dt$-embedding is thus causal. Across the full primary evaluation set, trained $\dt$-embedding triggers CIVO in 5/7 multi-joint locomotion seeds (71\%) versus 0/16 frozen or removed controls (Fisher exact $p < 0.001$). \emph{Tier 2 (negative control):} Single-joint Cartpole Swingup fails to trigger CIVO even with the identical trained $\dt$-embedding configuration (0/3 seeds, peak NVE $0.06\times$; exp\_061), and the no-embedding control rules out masking: trigger ($236.3$) and control ($244.8$) achieve comparable performance ($-65\%$ vs.\ $-64\%$ relative to baseline). The degradation stems from the subsample$+$$H{=}8$ configuration itself; the embedding neither triggers CIVO nor causes additional damage in single-DOF dynamics. \emph{Tier 3 (frozen-embedding specificity):} Three ManiSkill manipulation tasks (Table~\ref{tab:cross_domain}) show value-ratio transients at comparable magnitude ($4$--$11\times$), but frozen controls exhibit equal or higher peaks, ruling out $\dt$-embedding causation. Together, these tiers establish a scope boundary: CIVO is specific to multi-joint locomotion under imagination-based training with temporal conditioning, not a general pathology of $\dt$-conditioned world models.

